    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Driven west by the Oghuz, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a>, the Pechenegs drove the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a> west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a> by 892, pushing them towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a>, where the Magyars later founded the Hungarian state.</li><li>For more than two centuries they raided the lands of Rus'; in 972 they ambushed and killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sviatoslav-i-of-kiev">Sviatoslav I of Kiev</a>, and in 1036 Yaroslav I the Wise defeated them.</li><li>A combined Byzantine and Cuman army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexios-i-komnenos">Alexios I Komnenos</a> annihilated them as an independent force at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-levounion">Battle of Levounion</a> in 1091.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle">Primary Chronicle</a>, the Pecheneg Khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurya">Kurya</a> made a chalice from the skull of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sviatoslav-i-of-kiev">Sviatoslav I of Kiev</a>, in accordance with the custom of steppe nomads.</p></aside>
    </section>

