    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Pechenegs, called Patzinakoi by Byzantine writers and Bjnak or Bajanak in Arabic and Persian texts, were a semi-nomadic Turkic people who spoke the Pecheneg language. According to Omeljan Pritsak their homeland lay between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aral-sea">Aral Sea</a> and the middle course of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a>, on trade routes linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>, and they descended from the Kangars, listed among the subject peoples of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-turkic-khaganate">Eastern Turkic Khaganate</a>. Victor Spinei holds that the first certain reference to them is an 8th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghur</a> envoy's report, surviving in Tibetan, of fighting between the Be-ča-nag and the Hor near the Syr Darya.</p><p>After the Turkic Khaganate collapsed in 744, the Oghuz moved west against them. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-masudi">Al-Masudi</a> records that the Oghuz, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karluks">Karluks</a> and Kimaks defeated the Pechenegs near the Aral Sea before 850; most then migrated towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> and settled between the Ural and Volga rivers, while some joined the Oghuz. Attacked from two sides by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a> and the Oghuz, they moved west again across the Khazar Khaganate and expelled the Hungarians from the Kuban and the upper Donets. Pritsak dates this second migration to around 830, but Kristó suggests it could hardly have happened before the 850s.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Pechenegs&#8221; cites where it covers Origins and area:</p><ul><li>Victor Spinei, The Great Migrations in the East and South East of Europe from the Ninth to the Thirteenth Century (Translated by Dana Badulescu) (Romanian Cultural Institute, 2003)</li><li>Victor Spinei, The Romanians and the Turkic Nomads North of the Danube Delta from the Tenth to the Mid-Thirteenth century (Koninklijke Brill NV, 2009)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Muslim writers drawing on 9th-century sources describe the Pecheneg territory between the Ural and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> as a thirty-day walk across, bordered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oghuz-turks">Oghuz Turks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>. The Pechenegs raided their neighbours regularly, especially the Khazars and their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burtas">Burtas</a>, and sold their captives into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. Their 8th-century wars with the Oghuz were most probably fought for control of trade routes. When the Uzes later expelled them from their homeland, they seized most of their livestock and other goods.</p><p>Their language, which died out centuries ago, is known mainly from the names of their provinces recorded by Constantine Porphyrogenitus. Mahmud al-Kashgari thought it a variant of the Cuman and Oghuz idioms. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anna-komnene">Anna Komnene</a> said the Pechenegs and Cumans shared a common language. Scholars have placed it in the Oghuz or the Kipchak branch of the Turkic family, though it is too poorly documented to classify with certainty. Pecheneg communities later settled widely: around 150 villages in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a>, and places from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> still bear their name.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Pechenegs fought every neighbour in turn: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a>, Khazaria and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simeon-i-of-bulgaria">Simeon I of Bulgaria</a> employed them against the Magyars, and after the Battle of Southern Buh they drove the Magyars out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etelkoz">Etelköz</a> and the Pontic steppes. From the 9th century they raided the lands of Rus' for more than two centuries, sometimes in full-scale wars such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igor-of-kiev">Igor of Kiev</a>'s war on them in 920; at other times they allied with the Rus', as on Igor's Byzantine campaign of 943.</p><p>In 968 they besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>. Some joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sviatoslav-i-of-kiev">Sviatoslav I</a> in his Byzantine campaign of 970–971, but in 972 they ambushed and killed him. The fortunes of the war swung under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-the-great">Vladimir I of Kiev</a>, who founded the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pereiaslav">Pereyaslav</a> on the site of his victory over them, and Yaroslav I the Wise defeated them in 1036. Pecheneg <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> served under the Byzantines at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-manzikert">Battle of Manzikert</a>. A combined Byzantine and Cuman army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexios-i-komnenos">Alexios I Komnenos</a> annihilated them as an independent force at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-levounion">Battle of Levounion</a> in 1091, and the Byzantines defeated them again at the Battle of Beroia in 1122, on the territory of present-day Bulgaria.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-vii">Constantine VII Porphyrogenitus</a>, writing about 950, described Patzinakia as stretching west to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siret">Siret</a> River and divided into eight provinces under the same number of great princes, four tribes on each side of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a>, reflecting the Turkic division into left and right. The eight tribes were divided in turn into 40 sub-tribes, probably clans. He named the provinces Irtim, Tzour, Gyla, Koulpeï, Charaboï, Talmat, Chopon and Tzopon, and the princes who led them when they were expelled from their earlier lands. Peter Golden, following Németh and Ligeti, proposes that each tribal name joined an equine coat colour to the ruler's title.</p><p>Three of the tribes, the Erdim, Čur and Yula, formed the Kangars, deemed “more valiant and noble than the rest”. The late 10th-century Hudud al-'Alam distinguished the “Turkic Pechenegs” of the Donets from the “Khazarian Pechenegs” of the Kuban, probably those who accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazar</a> suzerainty. Later leaders known by name include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurya">Kurya</a> in the 970s, Metiga in the 980s and Kuchug in the 990s. Their statecraft has been judged to have gone little beyond attacks on neighbours and spells as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> for other powers.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Pechenegs helped redraw the map of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. By 892 they had driven the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a> west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a>, and their pressure made the Magyars leave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etelkoz">Etelköz</a> forever and settle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a>, where they later founded the Hungarian state. In the 9th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> allied with them to fend off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a> and the Magyars. Their wars against Kievan Rus' led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> of the Walachian lands to migrate north of the Dniestr in the 10th and 11th centuries. Byzantine emperors used Pecheneg troops again and again, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-manzikert">Battle of Manzikert</a> to the wars in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><p>Their remnants spread across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. One of the earliest introductions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a> came through an early 11th-century Muslim prisoner of the Byzantines who was brought among the Pechenegs and converted some of them, and in the late 12th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-hamid-al-gharnati">Abu Hamid al-Gharnati</a> wrote of Hungarian Pechenegs, probably Muslims, living disguised as Christians. In Hungary the title of Comes Bissenorum, Count of the Pechenegs, lasted for centuries, and some historians believe that Pechenegs contributed to the origins of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karakalpaks">Karakalpaks</a>.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Pechenegs&#8221; cites where it covers Language:</p><ul><li>Victor Spinei, The Great Migrations in the East and South East of Europe from the Ninth to the Thirteenth Century (Translated by Dana Badulescu) (Romanian Cultural Institute, 2003)</li><li>Victor Spinei, The Romanians and the Turkic Nomads North of the Danube Delta from the Tenth to the Mid-Thirteenth century (Koninklijke Brill NV, 2009)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After their defeat in 1036 the weakened Pechenegs were replaced on the Pontic steppe by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a> and the Torks; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mykhailo-hrushevsky">Mykhailo Hrushevsky</a> wrote that the horde moved to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, crossed it and disappeared from the Pontic steppes. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-levounion">Battle of Levounion</a> in 1091 Alexios I recruited the defeated Pechenegs and settled them in the district of Moglena as a tagma of the Moglena Pechenegs. The Cumans attacked them again in 1094, and many Pechenegs were slain or absorbed.</p><p>Pecheneg troops still fought for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>: at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otranto">Otranto</a> in 1105 or 1106 against Bohemond, and for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-i-komnenos">Manuel I Komnenos</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-i-of-sicily">William the Bad</a>, some of them at the Battle of Andria in 1155. The Pechenegs as a group were last mentioned in 1168, among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chorni-klobuky">Chorni Klobuky</a>, the “Black Hats”. Those south of the Danube were assimilated, mostly with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanians">Romanians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a>. The Pechenegs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> were probably decimated by the Mongol invasion, but names of Pecheneg origin went on appearing in documents, and in 15th-century Tolna county some people took the surname Besenyő. The village of Pečenjevce in southeast <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> was founded by Pechenegs who found refuge there after the war with Byzantium.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Pechenegs were a semi-nomadic Turkic people who left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> in the 9th century and dominated the steppes north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>. They drove the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a> west, served and fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a>, and were broken at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-levounion">Battle of Levounion</a> in 1091, after which their remnants were absorbed by their neighbours.</em></p>

